1002	    /**
1003	     * Extracts the lower-cased domain of an email — everything after its final {@code '@'}. This is
1004	     * the single definition of an email's domain, shared wherever an owner's email is matched by its
1005	     * domain (for example the disposable-domain blocklist applied in {@link #normalizeEmail(String)}),
1006	     * so every such rule reads the domain identically rather than re-deriving it. A {@code null} email
1007	     * (none was supplied) has no domain and yields {@code null}. Like {@link #canonicalEmail(String)}
1008	     * no validation is performed, so this can be applied to a value read back off an existing owner as
1009	     * readily as to one being normalized; a caller that needs a syntactically valid address validates
1010	     * it first. The domain is lower-cased so it compares case-insensitively, matching how
1011	     * {@link #DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS} is compared. For example {@code "jane@Example.COM"} yields
1012	     * {@code "example.com"}.
1013	     *
1014	     * @param email the email whose domain to extract, or {@code null} when none was supplied
1015	     * @return the lower-cased domain, or {@code null} when {@code email} is {@code null}
1016	     */
1017	    private String emailDomain(String email) {
1018	        if (email == null) {
1019	            return null;
1020	        }
1021	        return email.substring(email.lastIndexOf('@') + 1).toLowerCase(java.util.Locale.ROOT);
1022	    }
